// A `blank` pads the first segment to three actions so that a chain may
// follow it. The chain opens on an if/else, which is one statement costing
// two actions, and only one slot was left -- so it could not have been
// written in the first segment instead, which is the whole case for `blank`.
main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  int a = 1;
  int b = 2;
  blank;
} chain (int argc, string[] argv) {
  if (a > 0) {
    print(b);
  } else {
    print(a);
  }
} return int 0;
